mechanism:
convertNvvmIntrinsicToLlvm folds NVVM f16/f16x2 min/max/fma intrinsics to generic llvm.minimumnum/maximumnum/fma, gating on the module's denormal (FTZ) state:

  DenormalMode Mode = II->getFunction()->getDenormalMode(
      Action.IsHalfTy ? APFloat::IEEEhalf() : APFloat::IEEEsingle());
  bool FtzEnabled = Mode.Output == DenormalMode::PreserveSign;
  if (FtzEnabled != (Action.FtzRequirement == FTZ_MustBeOn))
    return nullptr;

For the f16/f16x2 cases IsHalfTy=true, so getDenormalMode(IEEEhalf()) returns the *default* denormal mode (Function::getDenormalMode only returns F32Mode for IEEEsingle; everything else returns DefaultMode). The fold's correctness rests on the assumption that, when it only fires in the ftz-state that matches the intrinsic, the backend will lower the resulting generic op with the *same* ftz-ness. That assumption is FALSE for f16. The backend fills the FTZFlag operand of every f16 min/max/fma instruction from useF32FTZ() (NVPTXISelLowering.cpp:146-149: getDenormalMode(IEEEsingle()).Output==PreserveSign), i.e. the *f32* (F32Mode) denormal mode -- see getFTZFlag in NVPTXInstrInfo.td:71-74 and FMA_F16/_f16_rr/_f16x2_rr which take FTZFlag:$ftz. So the fold keys on the half/default mode, the backend keys on the f32 mode. When those two modes disagree, the generic op is lowered with the wrong flush semantics. Forward: f32-ftz ON + half-ftz OFF -> non-ftz nvvm.fmin.f16 folds (half OFF matches FTZ_MustBeOff) -> backend emits flushing min.ftz.f16. Reverse: half-ftz ON + f32-ftz OFF -> nvvm.fmin.ftz.f16 folds (half ON matches FTZ_MustBeOn) -> backend emits non-flushing min.f16, dropping the required flush. bf16 variants are unaffected because their PTX ops have no $ftz operand.

