mechanism:
isLoweredToTrap() is documented as "a copy of the logic in SelectionDAGBuilder::visitUnreachable()". visitUnreachable now delegates to UnreachableInst::shouldLowerToTrap() (Instructions.cpp:4630), which contains:

  if (!TrapUnreachable) return false;
  if (CallInst *Call = ...; Call && Call->doesNotReturn()) {
    if (NoTrapAfterNoreturn) return false;
    if (Call->isNonContinuableTrap()) return false;
  }
  if (getFunction()->hasFnAttribute(Attribute::Naked)) return false;   // <-- present
  return true;

The NVPTX copy omits the Naked check entirely:
  if (auto *Call = dyn_cast_or_null<CallInst>(I.getPrevNode())) {
    if (Call->isNonContinuableTrap()) return true;
    if (NoTrapAfterNoreturn && Call->doesNotReturn()) return false;
  }
  return TrapUnreachable;   // returns true for a naked fn even though SelectionDAG emits no trap

Consequence: in a function with the `naked` attribute, with TrapUnreachable set, isLoweredToTrap() returns true, so runOnFunction() skips inserting the `exit;` inline-asm before the `unreachable` (line 147-148 `continue`). But SelectionDAGBuilder::visitUnreachable() -> shouldLowerToTrap() returns false for a naked function, so NO `trap` node is emitted either. The result is an `unreachable` block with no terminator: it falls through to the next block / off the end of the function. This pass exists specifically (PTXVersion < 8.3, NVPTXTargetMachine.cpp:400) to stop ptxas from inferring a spurious CFG edge out of an unreachable block; the Naked+TrapUnreachable combination defeats that and reintroduces exactly the divergent-CFG miscompile (e.g. bar.sync executed divergently) described in the file header. Over-inserting exit is harmless; UNDER-inserting (this case) is the miscompiling direction.

